  <section class="who" id="signals">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="section-label">AI intent data and business triggers</div>
      <h2>Use signals to decide where to investigate, not to pretend you can read a buyer's mind.</h2>
      <p class="ai-wide-copy">Modern B2B sales intelligence can surface useful changes and behaviors. Access depends on the client's systems, data sources, market, and selected tools. The most useful signal is one that can be connected to a sensible business reason for outreach.</p>
      <div class="ai-signal-table-wrap">
        <table class="ai-signal-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Signal category</th>
              <th>What it may tell us</th>
              <th>What the human still needs to confirm</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Hiring and job postings</strong></td>
              <td>A team may be growing, rebuilding, or investing in a capability.</td>
              <td>Whether the change creates a real need for your offer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Funding, expansion, or acquisition</strong></td>
              <td>The company may have a new mandate, budget pressure, integration work, or growth target.</td>
              <td>Who owns the priority and whether timing is relevant.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Leadership changes</strong></td>
              <td>A new executive may review vendors, systems, team structure, or performance.</td>
              <td>Whether the new leader is changing this specific area.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Technology changes</strong></td>
              <td>A new tool, replacement, migration, or integration may create a practical opening.</td>
              <td>Whether the data is current and the problem is active.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>First-party engagement</strong></td>
              <td>Website visits, content activity, replies, past conversations, or CRM movement may show interest.</td>
              <td>Who engaged, why they engaged, and whether the account fits.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Third-party intent data</strong></td>
              <td>Account-level research patterns may suggest attention around a relevant topic.</td>
              <td>Signal quality, buyer role, actual need, and readiness for a conversation.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Relevant company news</strong></td>
              <td>Restructuring, product launches, new markets, regulation, or operational change may create urgency.</td>
              <td>Whether the change connects to the problem you solve.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="ai-source-note">For context on current intent-data workflows, see <a href="https://6sense.com/platform/intent-data/what-is-intent-data/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">6sense's intent-data guide</a> and <a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/sales/ai-prospecting-agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">HubSpot's AI Prospecting Agent</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

